##DESCRIPTION
## A problem which asks for the smallest or largest standard deviation of three data sets. 
##ENDDESCRIPTION

## DBsubject(Statistics)
## DBchapter(Exploratory data analysis/descriptive statistics)
## DBsection(Summary statistics)
## Date(8/15/12)
## Institution(NAU)
## Author(C. Tyler Diggans)
## Level(2)
## KEYWORDS('five number summary')


DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'parserRadioButtons.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

@num1 = (1, 3, 4, 6, 7,  10);
@num2 = (1, 1, 5, 7, 12, 15);
@num3 = (1, 2, 3, 3, 3,  5);

$a = random(40, 90);
$b = random(5,  20);
$c = random(3,  7);

@nnum1 = map { $num1[$_] + $a } (0 .. $#num1);
@nnum2 = map { $num2[$_] + $b } (0 .. $#num2);
@nnum3 = map { $num3[$_] + $c } (0 .. $#num3);

@D   = ('largest', 'smallest');
@ans = ('B',       'C');
$f   = random(0, 1);

$rb = RadioButtons([ 'A', 'B', 'C' ], $ans[$f], separator => $SPACE x 2);

BEGIN_PGML

Data set A: [``` [@ join(", ", @nnum1)@] ```] 

Data set B: [``` [@ join(", ", @nnum2) @] ```] 

Data set C: [``` [@ join(", ", @nnum3) @] ```] 

Above are three different data sets. Without calculating anything, decide which set A, B, or C has the [$D[$f]] 
standard deviation. Just enter the letter of the data set as your answer.

[_]{$rb}
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Since we are looking for the spread of the data, and in this case looking for the [$D[$f]] standard deviation.  The standard deviation will be proportional to the range of the data.  In this case [$ans[$f]] has the [$D[$f]] range or spread. 
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
